SO ALL IN ALL:
Bob and Alice want to talk
Bob and Alice perform a diffie-hellman key exchange. They agree on a public variable, use it to calculate their private keys and public keys, exchange those keys, and then find a shared secret. (Note that the private and public keys here will later be used for RSA encryption)
Now, both Bob and Alice have their own private key, the other's public key, and the shared secret used for symmetric encryption.
Bob wants to send a document to Alice
Bob will encrypt the document with the shared secret and send it over the wire.
Bob will also take a hash of the document for integrity, and he will encrypt that hash with his private key that nobody else has, for verification.
Alice will decrypt Bob's hash with Bob's public key. If it decrypts, Alice knows that the message is, in fact, from Bob and not a mitm. 
Alice will then hash the file Bob sent over, and if the Hash's match, then she knows that she has the exact file Bob sent over. Integrity is verified. 
And, everything sent over the wire was done through a symmetric encryption key unknown to the man in the middle, that is only viable for the duration of their conversation.
